{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gs at exact clean pushed head 2a8b054b2f72932cf447efba1a07ed239a47c629, branch request/ancestry-test-cleanup. Fulfils request f9e833ad under promise 8003f3fa. Supersedes my rejected candidate 9a944e04 at this path.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE THAT REJECTION, WHICH YOU WERE RIGHT ABOUT. You found that my environment pins landed in newQueryFixture, which TestAncestryCostsTheSameWhateverTheNumberOfHeads never calls, so they could not touch the failing path. Confirmed. The cause was a non-unique three-line anchor and a replace-first-occurrence; the deeper fault was that both checks I ran to validate it -- the test passes, the mutation still bites -- were true regardless of where the edit landed, so neither could have caught a misplacement. I reported them as if they had.\n\nThis head resets to main and takes the whole previous change out. What remains is two lines inside the failing fixture, after its own init:\n\n  testGit(t, repo, \"config\", \"maintenance.auto\", \"false\")\n  testGit(t, repo, \"config\", \"gc.auto\", \"0\")\n\nRepository-local as you asked, so it binds this fixture and leaks into no other test. Nothing else: no sleep, no tolerated cleanup failure, no rm, no change to newQueryFixture or any other test.\n\nPLACEMENT, CHECKED THREE WAYS THIS TIME. The anchor was asserted unique before substitution, which is the check whose absence caused the first error. The diff hunk header names TestAncestryCostsTheSameWhateverTheNumberOfHeads. A scan of the file reports the enclosing function of each added line directly.\n\nTHE CLAIM IS UNTOUCHED. The two config calls run before the counting shim goes on PATH, so they are not tallied; the fixture still demands exactly two Git processes per invocation. Mutating classifyHeads to run one extra Git process per head still fails with \"ancestry ran 4 git processes for 2 heads and 66 for 64, want exactly 2 for each\".\n\nEPISTEMIC STATUS, UNCHANGED AND STILL NOT THE USUAL ONE. I have not reproduced